:: MoreGames [childhood]
# Learning Through Play

"Can you teach me more games?" you ask Saulā, eager to learn from the older child.

She smiles, pleased by your interest. "Of course! There are many games that help us learn important skills."

First, she teaches you a balancing game where you must walk along a fallen log without falling off. "This helps us learn to move carefully when crossing streams or narrow paths," she explains.

Next comes a memory game where several small objects are placed under a hide, shown briefly, then covered again. Players must remember what they saw and in what position. "Hunters need good memory to recall the paths they've taken and where animals were spotted," Saulā tells you.

Finally, she shows you a game involving throwing small sticks at a target. "This helps develop the aim we need for hunting with spears," she says, demonstrating the proper technique.

As you practice these games together, you realize that what seems like simple play is actually preparing you for adult skills. The tribe's children have been learning this way for countless generations.

